<Base title="Inside PostgreSQL" description="Follow one indexed read and committed update through PostgreSQL's backend process, buffers, heap versions, locks, WAL and cleanup.">
 <article class="visual-essay relational-design">
  <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage</p><h1>Inside PostgreSQL</h1><div class="essay-meta"><span>PostgreSQL 18</span><a href="/learn/databases/relational/">Relational introduction ↗</a></div></header>
  <p>PostgreSQL turns tables and transactions into a running system of processes, memory, data pages and log records. A SQL statement describes an answer or a change. The server must choose how to find the records, decide which versions the caller can see, coordinate competing changes and preserve a committed result through a crash.</p>
  <p>These mechanisms meet on an ordinary request. We will find one product and reserve two units of its stock. Following that small operation connects the query plan to the stored row, and the transaction’s success response to the machinery that can recover it.</p>
  <p>The explanation concerns a conventional PostgreSQL 18 server with ordinary logged tables. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=pg-read-write">browser lesson</a> uses PGlite to execute the SQL and inspect plans. Its embedded process and storage environment do not reproduce the server’s separate processes, network or physical-disk guarantees.</p>
  <nav aria-label="In this article"><ol><li><a href="#connection">The connection and backend</a></li><li><a href="#read">From SQL to a row</a></li><li><a href="#change">Changing the record</a></li><li><a href="#commit">Commit and persistent storage</a></li><li><a href="#cleanup">The work that follows</a></li></ol></nav>

  <section id="connection"><h2>The connection and backend</h2>
   <p>An application connects to the PostgreSQL server and establishes a session. In the conventional server architecture, a <strong>backend process</strong> serves that connection. It receives commands, plans and executes them, and sends rows or errors back. The application may borrow this connection from a pool, but the database session still belongs to that backend while the connection exists.</p>
   <p>Several backends can work on the same database. They share structures including buffers that hold database pages in memory and mechanisms for coordinating locks and transaction state. Each backend also has private working memory, such as state for a sort or hash operation. Opening another connection creates another participant; it does not give that participant a private copy of every table.</p>
   <p>The server also runs background processes. A checkpointer coordinates periodic flushing of changed data pages, a background writer helps make buffers reusable, a WAL writer flushes log data, and autovacuum workers maintain tables. Their work overlaps with application requests. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/tutorial-arch.html">architecture introduction</a> describes the client/backend arrangement; memory settings distinguish <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-resource.html">shared buffers from per-operation working memory</a>.</p>
   <p>For our example, the application sends a product lookup and awaits its response. The table has 10,000 products with an integer primary key, a name and available stock. Product 7 begins with three available units.</p>
   <Sql code={read} label="The application asks for product 7" />
  </section>

  <section id="read"><h2>From SQL to a row</h2>
   <p>PostgreSQL parses the statement and resolves its names and types using system catalogues. Rewriting can expand a view into the underlying query. The planner then considers supported access paths and estimates their work from the query, indexes, statistics and configuration. The executor follows the chosen plan. These are separate stages along the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/query-path.html">path of a query</a>; SQL text does not directly instruct the storage system to read a particular page.</p>
   <p>The primary key has a B-tree index. For one product among ten thousand, the lesson’s plan uses that index. Its ordered entries let the executor locate key 7 without scanning every product. The matching entry leads to a candidate row version in the table’s main storage, the <strong>heap</strong>.</p>
   <p>The heap is divided into pages, usually 8 KiB in a standard PostgreSQL build. A row version occupies space on a page and has a small item identifier pointing to it. A tuple location, exposed as <code>ctid</code>, combines the page number with the item identifier’s position. A location such as <code>(0,7)</code> means an item on page zero; it is not the product’s identity and can change when the row is updated.</p>
   <p>The backend accesses the needed index and heap pages through shared buffers. If a page is already there, it can use that memory copy. Otherwise it requests the page through the storage layer; the operating system may also have it cached. A buffer read counter therefore does not, by itself, establish a physical disk read.</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Product lookup through PostgreSQL"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Information used</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Plan the lookup</td><td>Primary-key index, table statistics, equality condition</td><td>An index scan for product 7</td></tr><tr><td>Search index pages</td><td>Ordered product keys</td><td>A candidate heap location</td></tr><tr><td>Visit heap page</td><td>Page number and item identifier</td><td>A candidate row version with its values and visibility metadata</td></tr><tr><td>Check visibility and condition</td><td>Query snapshot and transaction state</td><td>The version allowed in this query’s answer</td></tr><tr><td>Return selected fields</td><td>Visible product row</td><td>Product 7, its name, available 3</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
   <p>Finding the key is not sufficient to decide visibility. PostgreSQL can retain an earlier version for an older reader while a later reader sees a changed value. Row headers and transaction information let the executor decide which version belongs in this query’s <strong>snapshot</strong>, its view of committed work. An ordinary Read Committed SELECT takes a statement snapshot; it does not continually change its answer as other transactions commit during that statement.</p>
   <p>An index-only scan can sometimes avoid the heap when the index supplies every requested value and the visibility map establishes that the relevant page’s tuples are visible to all transactions. Our query asks for name and available, while the primary-key index contains the key and row references, so an ordinary heap fetch supplies the missing fields. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/row-versions/">row-version article</a> explores how updates and vacuum change that eligibility.</p>
   <p>A very wide value can also be compressed or stored out of line through PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/storage-toast.html">TOAST mechanism</a>. A heap row can then contain a reference to that storage. “Fetch one row” does not promise that every value in it lives on one page, which is one reason to request only fields the application needs.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="change"><h2>Changing the record</h2>
   <p>The application now attempts to reserve two units. It sends a conditional update inside a transaction and checks the returned rows before proceeding. The bounded lesson commits just this stock change; a real checkout also records the purchase within its chosen transaction boundary.</p>
   <Sql code={reserve} label="Reserve only if at least two units remain" />
   <p>The condition and subtraction belong to the same update. PostgreSQL locates product 7, establishes which version it can update and coordinates access to that row. If another uncommitted updater holds it, this operation may wait. At Read Committed, after a competing updater commits, PostgreSQL can re-evaluate the condition on the updated version. If only one unit then remains, the reservation returns no row.</p>
   <p>In our uncontended run, three units satisfy the guard and the new value is one. PostgreSQL generally creates a <strong>new row version</strong> for the update instead of overwriting the old version’s values in place. The logical product ID stays 7, while the visible <code>ctid</code> changes. Older snapshots can still need the earlier version. The update therefore changes both user data and the metadata used to determine visibility.</p>
   <p>Index maintenance depends on the change. When indexed values do not change and the new version fits on the same heap page, a <strong>HOT</strong> update can avoid creating ordinary new index entries by linking heap versions. Other updates need index work. The stock field here is not in the primary-key index, making HOT possible, but space and actual layout decide whether it happens. The lesson observes the version change without claiming a particular HOT outcome.</p>
   <p>The modified pages in buffers become <strong>dirty</strong>: their memory contents differ from their stored versions. PostgreSQL also generates WAL describing the logged changes. Neither a dirty heap page nor an emitted WAL record alone means the transaction has committed. Until the transaction resolves, its locks and version state still constrain other work.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="commit"><h2>Commit and persistent storage</h2>
   <p>For an ordinary durable local commit, with <code>fsync=on</code>, <code>synchronous_commit=on</code> and storage honouring flush requests, PostgreSQL makes the transaction’s required WAL durable before acknowledging success. It does not need to flush every changed heap and index page at that moment. WAL provides the information needed to reconstruct those logged changes if a crash interrupts the later page writes.</p>
   <p>This is <strong>write-ahead logging</strong>: the required log must reach durable storage before a corresponding changed data page is written there. A committed transaction can therefore be durable while some of its data pages are still only changed in memory. Conversely, a data page may contain physical effects of a transaction that did not commit; visibility and transaction status determine whether those versions form part of a query’s answer.</p>
   <p>A checkpoint coordinates a recoverable boundary by getting required dirty pages written and recording checkpoint information. After a crash, PostgreSQL finds an appropriate checkpoint and replays WAL from its redo position. It can reconstruct logged page changes and restore transaction-consistent visibility without needing every transaction to have forced all its own pages at commit.</p>
   <p>The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/recovery/">WAL and recovery article</a> follows the order of those events, the difference between insertion and flush positions, and how asynchronous commit changes the success promise. A browser COMMIT response cannot verify that a server’s drive or storage service honours the required flushes.</p>
   <p>Even on a correctly configured server, a committed result does not establish that the application received the reply. The connection could disappear after the commit became durable. The handler must reconcile that uncertain outcome using its request identity, rather than assume failure and repeat the purchase blindly.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="cleanup"><h2>The work that follows</h2>
   <p>Product 7 now has one available unit. A new ordinary read can see that committed value, and another attempt to reserve two returns no row. The earlier version may still be present physically. Once no relevant snapshot or other retention requirement needs it, PostgreSQL can reclaim its space through page pruning and vacuum.</p>
   <p>Vacuum also maintains visibility information and protects transaction-ID reuse by freezing sufficiently old tuple information. ANALYZE has a different job: it samples data for planner statistics. Autovacuum can schedule both kinds of work. Their importance follows directly from the read and write path: stale estimates can choose unsuitable plans, retained versions consume space, and lost all-visible bits can require more heap visits.</p>
   <p>This makes a long transaction an operational concern even when it sends little SQL. A retained snapshot can keep earlier versions relevant and delay cleanup. Adding more storage does not end that snapshot, just as adding an index does not release another transaction’s lock.</p>
   <p>The server has now carried one request through several kinds of state: an application session, a planned access path, buffered pages, visible row versions, transaction locks, durable log and eventual cleanup. When behaviour changes, those distinctions help locate the cause. A slow query, a waiting update and a delayed cleanup are connected, but they are not the same event.</p>
  </section>
